    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Sultan, qadi and the elders of the town",
    text: "Each Swahili town was independent, governed by a sultan or by councils of elders from the leading patrician lineages. Kilwa's sultans, who claimed descent from Shirazi Persians, built the great palace of Husuni Kubwa on a cliff above the sea in the fourteenth century. Law was Islamic, of the Shafi'i school: a qadi heard disputes over marriage, inheritance and debt, while much else was settled by elders and heads of wards. Quarrels between patrician families could split a town for generations into rival moieties that competed in dances, poetry and processions. After the Omani takeover in the nineteenth century, governors (liwali) represented the sultan of Zanzibar in each town.",
