    text: "Swahili children began Qur'an school (chuo) at about five, sitting on mats in a teacher's house or a mosque veranda to learn Arabic letters and recite the Qur'an by heart; finishing the Qur'an was celebrated with a feast. Girls attended as well as boys, though patrician girls were kept increasingly indoors from puberty. Boys of merchant families learned trade by accompanying fathers and uncles, keeping accounts and sailing with the monsoon fleets; sons of craftsmen learned carpentry or masonry from masters. A gifted student might go on to study law and theology with a sheikh, or travel to Hadramawt or Mecca. Poetry, proverbs and the rules of polite speech were part of every child's schooling.",
