    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Burial by nightfall and the widow's eda",
    text: "A Swahili Muslim was washed, wrapped in white cloth and buried the same day, facing Mecca, in a cemetery outside the mosque or, for the great, in a pillar tomb. Pillar tombs, tall stone columns set with imported porcelain bowls, stand near the ruined mosques of the coast and mark the graves of important men. Readings of the Qur'an (hitima) were held for the dead on the third day and the fortieth, and families gave alms in their name. A widow observed the eda of four months and ten days, staying indoors, avoiding perfume and ornaments, and not remarrying; afterward she could marry again. Islamic inheritance gave her a fixed share, an eighth if there were children.",
