    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Stone town and makuti town",
    text: "Every Swahili town had two parts: the stone town of the patricians, with multi-storey coral houses around courtyards, and the much larger town of mud-and-wattle houses roofed with makuti, thatch of plaited coconut fronds, where commoners, clients and slaves lived. A makuti roof lasted only a few years and was redone in the dry season with help from neighbours. Towns were founded, according to their chronicles, by migrants from Shiraz who bought land from local rulers; archaeology shows the towns grew from local African fishing and farming villages that converted to Islam and gained foreign traders. Founding a house or a town required prayers and a sacrifice at the foundation.",
    wiki: ["Makuti", "Swahili architecture", "Shirazi people"],
