    topics: [{ goal: "food-store" }, { occasion: "day.check-store" }],
    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Rice, coconut and dried fish in the storeroom",
    text: "The Swahili diet centred on rice, sorghum and millet, cooked with coconut milk and eaten with fish, vegetables and on feast days meat, spiced with cloves, cardamom and pepper from the Indian Ocean trade. Patrician houses had storerooms at the back or on the ground floor for grain, dried fish and imported goods, kept in large jars. Grain came from the town's own shambas and from the mainland and Madagascar, and a failed harvest meant buying from elsewhere at high prices. Salted or sun-dried fish and shark kept well. Checking the store meant sieving grain for weevils and seeing that jars of ghee, honey and coconut oil were sealed against ants.",
