    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Hoe, mulch and the permanent garden",
    text: "Around the northern and western shores of Lake Victoria, where rain fell in two seasons and never failed for long, people grew bananas in permanent gardens that could be worked for generations. Women tended them with iron hoes, cutting old stems, mulching with the leaves and replanting suckers; men cleared new land, built fences and grew the beer bananas. Beyond the banana zone, in drier Bunyoro, Busoga and Ankole, finger millet, sorghum, beans and sweet potatoes were the staple field crops, sown with a hoe at the start of each rain. By the late nineteenth century Arab traders had introduced rice and missionaries new crops; colonial officials pushed cotton after 1904 and coffee after that, which peasants grew on their own plots.",
