    title: "Hima herdsmen and the long-horned cattle",
    text: "Cattle in the Lakes region meant the Hima and Tutsi, herding peoples with long-horned Ankole cattle whose huge curved horns were bred for display. In Ankole and Rwanda, herders were the ruling class and cattle were the measure of wealth and honour; herders moved between wet- and dry-season pastures and lived mainly on milk. In banana-growing Buganda the kabaka and chiefs owned large herds but hired Hima herdsmen to look after them, since the Ganda themselves were cultivators. Cattle were milked by men into wooden pots, and butter was used to anoint the body. Rinderpest in the 1890s killed most of the region's cattle and ruined many herding families.",
