    title: "Canoe fleets of the lake and the hunters of the bush",
    text: "Lake Victoria was a highway and a larder. The Ganda and the Ssese islanders built large plank canoes sewn with fibre, with a long prow ending in a horn-shaped beak, paddled by crews of twenty or more; the kabaka kept fleets of hundreds for war and tribute collection. Fishermen worked from smaller canoes with nets of fibre, hooks and basket traps, and smoked or dried the catch for sale. The Ssese islands were also home to the shrine of Mukasa, god of the lake, whose priests had to be consulted before voyages. On land, hunters took antelope, buffalo and elephant with spears, nets and pit traps, and specialist hunters supplied ivory for the growing Arab and Swahili trade in the nineteenth century.",
