    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Salt of Kibiro, iron of Bunyoro and the clan crafts",
    text: "The Lakes kingdoms traded in minerals from particular places. At Kibiro on Lake Albert, women scraped salt-bearing earth, leached it with water, and boiled the brine into salt cakes; salt was also collected from the crater lake at Katwe. Bunyoro's smiths were famous for iron hoes and spears, which traded widely. In Buganda, many crafts were the hereditary duties of particular clans: some clans made barkcloth for the kabaka, others canoes, drums or pots, and a clan's craft was part of its identity. Smiths and drum-makers commanded respect and ritual power: a royal drum had to be made with ceremony, and forging tools for the king involved offerings to guard against spirits.",
    wiki: ["Kibiro", "Lake Katwe", "Clans of Baganda"],
