    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Burial in the garden and the installing of the heir",
    text: "A Ganda was buried in the banana garden of the homestead, wrapped in barkcloth, with the mourners wailing, fasting and refraining from work. The spirit of the dead remained near the grave. Months later, the clan held the last funeral rites (okwabya olumbe, 'breaking the death'), at which the heir, chosen by the clan elders and not necessarily the eldest son, was installed with barkcloth and a spear, and the mourning ended with feasting. The heir took responsibility for the dead man's children and widows, whom he might inherit as wives, though a widow could also return to her kin. For a kabaka the jawbone, thought to hold the spirit, was kept in a shrine.",
    wiki: ["Baganda", "Kasubi Tombs", "Widow inheritance"],
