    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Gardens, herds and the fish that was not eaten",
    text: "In Zululand and the lands of the Xhosa, women hoed gardens of sorghum, maize (grown widely by the eighteenth century), pumpkins and beans, and stored grain in underground pits beneath the cattle byre. Men and boys herded; cattle were the basis of marriage, sacrifice and status, and a man's standing was counted in them. Hunting was a man's pursuit, often in great organised drives by the king's regiments, and gave meat and skins for dress. Fish, despite rivers and a long coast, were widely avoided as food among Zulu and Xhosa alike. Iron was smelted by specialist smiths from local ores, clay was dug for pots, and red ochre was mined for body paint.",
    wiki: ["Zulu people", "Xhosa people", "Nguni cattle"],
