    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Smiths, beadwork and the Delagoa Bay trade",
    text: "Southern Nguni and Zulu smiths forged hoes and assegai blades in secluded places away from women, since smelting was surrounded by medicines and sexual taboos; a good smith's work was valued by kings and they were among the most honoured of craftsmen. Women made pots, baskets and, after glass beads arrived through trade, elaborate beadwork whose colours and patterns carried messages. There were no markets: goods moved as gifts, bridewealth and tribute, and by barter. From the eighteenth century, ivory, cattle and hides went east to Portuguese and other traders at Delagoa Bay in exchange for beads and brass, which Zulu kings controlled. Everything was carried: women on their heads, boys as carriers for the regiments.",
