    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Inkundla, induna and the praise-poet",
    text: "In a Zulu or Xhosa chiefdom, disputes were heard in the open at the inkundla, the meeting place at the gate of the chief's homestead, where men sat in a circle and any could question the parties. Lesser matters went to the homestead head or a local induna, a headman appointed by the chief. Fines were paid in cattle, and the king's court under Shaka and his successors at royal kraals like Dukuza and Ulundi dealt with serious crimes, sometimes with death. News came by runners and messengers, and was spread and shaped by the imbongi, praise-poets who recited the deeds, and also the faults, of chiefs in public. Neighbours in a district knew one another as kin, in-laws or age-mates, and helped at harvests and ceremonies.",
