    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Beehive huts around the byre",
    text: "A Zulu homestead (umuzi) was a ring of beehive-shaped huts of grass thatch over a frame of bent saplings, arranged in a circle around the cattle byre, facing downhill toward the gate. At the top stood the great hut of the homestead head's mother or chief wife; other wives' huts were ranked left and right. Each wife had her own hut, garden and granary, cooked for her children and served her husband in turn. A young married man set up his own homestead when he could afford it, with his father's blessing, and new wives' huts were added as a man prospered. Women re-smeared floors with cow dung, and men renewed thatch; building was done by neighbours working together.",
