    text: "Nguni children learned by doing. Boys herded calves and then cattle from about six, living much of the day in the veld with other boys, learning to fight with sticks, hunt birds and know every beast by its colour and horns; older boys disciplined younger ones. Girls cared for younger children, fetched water and wood, and worked in the gardens. Xhosa youths went through circumcision schools (ulwaluko) in the bush; among the Zulu, Shaka abolished circumcision and young men were instead enrolled in age regiments. A girl's first menstruation was marked with seclusion and a feast, and later her coming of age as marriageable (umemulo). Children learned avoidance speech (hlonipha) as marks of respect toward elders and in-laws.",
