    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Beer and beef for the amadlozi",
    text: "Zulu and Xhosa religion turned on the ancestors (amadlozi, amathongo), who watched over their descendants and sent misfortune if neglected. At the back of the great hut, the umsamo, beer was set aside for them and they were addressed by name. At every important event, a birth, a marriage, a return from war, an illness, a goat or ox was slaughtered in the byre, where the ancestors were thought to dwell, and its bellowing called them. Unkulunkulu, the first ancestor, was a distant creator rather than an object of daily prayer. Christian missions arrived from the 1830s; many converts later joined African-led churches that kept regard for the ancestors alongside the Bible.",
