    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Ethiopian churches, night schools and the African press",
    text: "Johannesburg's Africans built religious institutions of their own. In 1892 Mangena Mokone, a Methodist minister on the Rand, broke away to found the Ethiopian Church, the first of many African-led churches that rejected white control; later Zionist churches, with healing, prophecy and white robes, grew among migrants. Congregations met in rooms, halls and open veld on Sundays, and gave offerings for their church and ministers. Mission night schools taught workers to read English and arithmetic, the route to jobs as clerks, interpreters and teachers. Newspapers in African languages and English, like Imvo Zabantsundu from the Cape and Abantu-Batho, founded in Johannesburg in 1912, carried news, politics and advertisements, and were read aloud in the compounds.",
