    title: "Tsodilo: glittering hematite and the herders' cattle",
    text: "The Kalahari was never sealed off. At the Tsodilo Hills in the northwest, people mined specularite, a glittering hematite, from shafts in the rock between about 800 and 1100 CE, and traded it as a cosmetic; the same sites held iron, copper, glass beads and marine shells, showing links reaching to the Atlantic and Indian Ocean coasts. Farmers and herders, ancestors of Tswana and other Bantu speakers in the east and Khoe herders in the west, kept cattle and goats where water allowed, and grew sorghum and melons at the desert margins. Foragers traded meat, skins and ostrich eggshell beads for iron, tobacco and milk, and worked for herders as clients. Loads were carried on the back in skin bags and nets.",
    wiki: ["Tsodilo", "Hematite", "Tswana people"],
