    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Grass shelters, the fire and talk until morning",
    text: "A !Kung camp was a circle of small grass shelters, each built by a woman in an hour or two from branches and grass, facing a central space, with a family hearth in front of each. Camps moved several times a year to follow water and food, and a new camp was made in a morning; shelters were not mended so much as rebuilt. There was little to store: meat was eaten or dried into strips, nuts were carried in skin bags, and water was kept in buried ostrich eggshells. There were no chiefs: a band had a senior owner of its water and territory (n!ore), but decisions were made by talk around the fires, often through the night, and serious quarrels were settled by one party moving to another band.",
