    text: "Kalahari foragers made nearly all their gear from what was around them: bows of grewia wood strung with sinew, reed arrows tipped with bone or iron and smeared with beetle-larva poison, digging sticks, skin bags, and carrying nets. Women made beads from ostrich eggshell, chipping and drilling fragments, then grinding them smooth in strings; these beads, made for at least forty thousand years in southern Africa, were worn and given in hxaro exchange. After summer rains the pans filled, and people camped near them to hunt game that came to drink and to catch frogs and terrapins. The power in objects came from their makers and from exchange: a gift carried a relationship with it.",
