    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Children in the play group, widows in the band",
    text: "!Kung children were carried by their mothers until three or four and nursed long, and then joined mixed-age play groups in the camp, where they learned by imitation: boys shot at beetles with tiny bows, girls dug roots and played at house. There was little formal teaching and no punishment to speak of; a boy's first large kill was marked by a ritual of scarification, and a girl's first menstruation by a dance. A married couple usually began living with the bride's parents while the husband did bride service, then chose where to live. A widow stayed with her own or her husband's kin, and could remarry after a period of mourning; her children belonged to the band that raised them.",
